analysisWe need to produce assert statement for comment after set method.

Method signature: public Object set(int index, Object element)

Implementation: if (!tainted) tainted = isTainted(element);
if (typeSafe) checkTypeSafe(element);
return super.set(index, element);

Comment: "@@@ If the index is non-negative and less than the initial size of the list, then the result is equal to the element at that index before the operation."

We need assert statement capturing this specification.

We need to refer to initial size: use \old(this.size()).

Result is return value: \result.

Condition: index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)))? Actually get returns Role, but set returns Object (previous element). Since list holds Objects (Roles). So we can assert equality using == or .equals? In examples they used == for primitive, .equals for objects. Since the element could be any Object, we can just use ==? In spec they said "result is equal to the element at that index before the operation". Could be equality of objects (reference equality). Use ==.

Thus assert: index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \result == \old(this.get(index));

But need parentheses due to precedence. Use (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => (\result == \old(this.get(index)));

Thus final code:

<code>assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => (\result == \old(this.get(index)));</code>

Check if need to use this.get(index) before operation: \old(this.get(index)). Yes.

Return type is Object, so equality using == is fine.

